L["tempest"] = """It had been a long day. Jesus had been teaching crowds beside the Sea of Galilee, telling parables from a boat pushed out a little from the shore. When evening came, he said to his disciples, let us go over to the other side. Leaving the crowd behind, they took him along in the boat, just as he was. Other boats were with them.

The Sea of Galilee sits low among hills, and cold air rushing down can whip up violent storms without warning. Several of the disciples were experienced fishermen who knew this lake well. A furious squall came up, and the waves broke over the boat, so that it was nearly swamped. These were professionals, and they were afraid.

And where was Jesus? He was in the stern, sleeping on a cushion. He was exhausted from the day, and he slept through the storm. The disciples woke him and said, teacher, don't you care if we drown?

He got up, rebuked the wind and said to the waves, quiet! Be still! Then the wind died down and it was completely calm. He said to his disciples, why are you so afraid? Do you still have no faith? They were terrified and asked each other, who is this? Even the wind and the waves obey him!

Their first fear was of the storm. Their second fear, once it was calm, was of the man who had stopped it. Mark says they were filled with great fear. They had just seen something that only God does.

Why does this matter? The Psalms say it is the Lord who stills the roaring of the seas, the roaring of their waves. The disciples knew those songs. When Jesus spoke to the sea and it obeyed, they began to realize who was in their boat. He was a real man who got tired and fell asleep, and he was also the Lord of creation.

What happened after? They reached the other side of the lake, where Jesus healed a man who had been suffering terribly. The disciples' question, who is this, kept getting answered through the rest of the Gospels. Later, Peter would say, you are the Messiah, the Son of the living God.

What does this mean for us today? The disciples were in the storm because Jesus had told them to cross. Obedience doesn't guarantee smooth water. Storms come to the faithful too. It also shows that Jesus is with us in the boat. He isn't far away. And though the disciples woke him with a blunt question, don't you care, he answered them anyway. We can bring our fear to him, even when it comes out sharp.

Notice also what he asked. He didn't ask why they were in danger. He asked why they were afraid, and where their faith was. The point was never only the storm. It was trust in the one who was with them.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus has authority over everything that frightens us. And it invites us to say, with the disciples, who is this? The answer is the Son of God, who can speak peace to the wildest waves."""
